WEBVTT

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:22.773
[David Attenborough] <i>Just 50 years ago,</i>

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.276
<i>we finally ventured to the moon.</i>

00:00:40.332 --> 00:00:45.796
<i>For the very first time,</i>
<i>we looked back at our own planet.</i>

00:00:53.387 --> 00:00:58.684
<i>Since then, the human population</i>
<i>has more than doubled.</i>

00:01:04.231 --> 00:01:08.652
<i>This series will celebrate</i>
<i>the natural wonders that remain</i>

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.281
<i>and reveal what we must preserve</i>

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:16.868
<i>to ensure people and nature thrive.</i>

00:01:30.674 --> 00:01:33.844
<i>The Earth still has sanctuaries,</i>

00:01:35.554 --> 00:01:40.892
<i>and, on occasion, they hold</i>
<i>spectacular gatherings of wildlife.</i>

00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:52.362
<i>They provide vital space,</i>
<i>but they're disappearing fast.</i>

00:02:00.621 --> 00:02:01.913
[wildebeest lowing]

00:03:08.146 --> 00:03:12.734
<i>A fifth of the land </i>
<i>on our planet is covered by desert.</i>

00:03:19.116 --> 00:03:23.954
<i>The driest of all</i>
<i>is the Atacama in South America.</i>

00:03:27.541 --> 00:03:31.503
<i>There are places here</i>
<i>where rain has never been recorded.</i>

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:47.894
<i>Deserts may appear to be barren and empty,</i>

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.776
<i>but they are of crucial importance</i>
<i>to life.</i>

00:04:11.042 --> 00:04:13.670
<i>For those that can overcome</i>
<i>their challenges,</i>

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:17.215
<i>they provide a vital refuge.</i>

00:04:20.969 --> 00:04:22.971
[wind whistling]

00:04:32.189 --> 00:04:34.024
<i>Socotran cormorants,</i>

00:04:34.775 --> 00:04:38.904
<i>emerging from a dust storm</i>
<i>in the Arabian desert.</i>

00:04:46.661 --> 00:04:52.000
<i>It is the very emptiness of this landscape</i>
<i>that has brought them here,</i>

00:04:55.837 --> 00:04:59.299
<i>and they have come in immense numbers.</i>

00:05:01.051 --> 00:05:03.053
[birds squawking]

00:05:06.056 --> 00:05:07.933
<i>Fifty thousand of them.</i>

00:05:13.563 --> 00:05:16.149
<i>A quarter of the total population.</i>

00:05:24.157 --> 00:05:27.369
<i>They have come</i>
<i>because here they can breed...</i>

00:05:28.578 --> 00:05:29.955
<i>undisturbed.</i>

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:48.181
<i>But nesting in a desert is difficult.</i>

00:05:50.433 --> 00:05:53.937
<i>Temperatures can reach 40 degrees Celsius.</i>

00:05:56.314 --> 00:05:59.192
<i>Yet, both the adults</i>
<i>and their white chicks</i>

00:05:59.276 --> 00:06:01.194
<i>are ready for this challenge.</i>

00:06:01.278 --> 00:06:03.280
[birds panting]

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.201
<i>They cool themselves </i>
<i>by panting.</i>

00:06:16.126 --> 00:06:20.797
<i>Any adult that appears to have food</i>
<i>in its crop is mobbed.</i>

00:06:24.342 --> 00:06:27.304
<i>An adult will only give food</i>
<i>to its own chick,</i>

00:06:29.598 --> 00:06:31.349
<i>which must be here somewhere.</i>

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:46.615
<i>The chicks chase an adult</i>
<i>out into the desert.</i>

00:06:54.497 --> 00:06:55.332
<i>No luck.</i>

00:06:56.499 --> 00:07:00.295
<i>Now, they must get back quickly</i>
<i>to the safety of the colony.</i>

00:07:09.304 --> 00:07:11.932
<i>This desert provides the cormorants</i>

00:07:12.015 --> 00:07:14.935
<i>with more than just a secure refuge.</i>

00:07:31.326 --> 00:07:34.996
<i>Every morning, a mass movement begins.</i>

00:07:47.676 --> 00:07:49.177
<i>It's the rush hour.</i>

00:08:14.327 --> 00:08:17.998
<i>A shallow arm of the sea,</i>
<i>right beside the colony,</i>

00:08:18.331 --> 00:08:20.083
<i>is full of food.</i>

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:22.502
[cawing]

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:27.215
<i>This richness comes</i>
<i>from the desert itself.</i>

00:08:28.591 --> 00:08:30.552
<i>Dust, blown from the land,</i>

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:35.181
<i>contains nutrients</i>
<i>that fertilize the surrounding waters.</i>

00:08:44.607 --> 00:08:49.571
<i>So, it is the desert itself</i>
<i>that enriches the sea.</i>

00:09:16.473 --> 00:09:22.520
<i>In Oman, during the summer monsoon,</i>
<i>fogs roll in from the sea,</i>

00:09:23.521 --> 00:09:26.107
<i>billowing over the Dhofar mountains.</i>

00:09:34.574 --> 00:09:39.579
<i>The mists bring just enough moisture</i>
<i>to sustain a little vegetation...</i>

00:09:47.087 --> 00:09:51.883
<i>and this sparse greenery</i>
<i>becomes a focus for life.</i>

00:09:59.557 --> 00:10:06.106
<i>Ibex must cross the near vertical cliffs</i>
<i>to reach one of the few springs.</i>

00:10:09.776 --> 00:10:10.985
<i>But they're nervous...</i>

00:10:13.029 --> 00:10:14.572
<i>and with good reason.</i>

00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:24.499
<i>An Arabian leopard,</i>

00:10:25.834 --> 00:10:29.879
<i>one of less than 200</i>
<i>that survive in the wild.</i>

00:10:33.508 --> 00:10:35.218
<i>This male's territory</i>

00:10:35.301 --> 00:10:42.267
<i>extends over 350 square kilometers</i>
<i>of high mountains and deep wadis.</i>

00:10:51.734 --> 00:10:54.404
<i>The southern edge</i>
<i>of the Arabian Peninsula</i>

00:10:54.904 --> 00:10:58.408
<i>is one of the few places left</i>
<i>where there is enough prey</i>

00:10:58.867 --> 00:11:02.036
<i>to sustain a population of these leopards.</i>

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:12.380
<i>But even so, there are probably</i>
<i>less than 60 individuals</i>

00:11:13.047 --> 00:11:16.176
<i>in over 15,000 square kilometers.</i>

00:11:23.516 --> 00:11:26.561
<i>These leopards have always been rare,</i>

00:11:29.981 --> 00:11:32.025
<i>but now conflict with people</i>

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:35.695
<i>is causing their numbers</i>
<i>to decline still further.</i>

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:38.990
[bird chirps]

00:11:49.834 --> 00:11:51.502
<i>Few leopards are left,</i>

00:11:52.629 --> 00:11:58.051
<i>so what our hidden cameras now capture</i>
<i>verges on the miraculous.</i>

00:12:05.475 --> 00:12:06.893
<i>A female leopard,</i>

00:12:08.811 --> 00:12:11.189
<i>and she is tracking a male.</i>

00:12:26.788 --> 00:12:30.375
<i>Such meetings</i>
<i>are becoming increasingly rare.</i>

00:12:50.103 --> 00:12:51.854
[growling]

00:12:55.608 --> 00:12:57.902
<i>This briefest of unions</i>

00:12:58.361 --> 00:13:02.532
<i>may ensure</i>
<i>the short-term future for these leopards.</i>

00:13:08.454 --> 00:13:09.956
<i>But in the longer term,</i>

00:13:10.915 --> 00:13:14.294
<i>their fate will depend</i>
<i>on their territory being protected.</i>

00:13:22.051 --> 00:13:24.262
<i>To the north of the Dhofar mountains</i>

00:13:24.971 --> 00:13:29.058
<i>lies a place</i>
<i>of almost unimaginable emptiness.</i>

00:13:30.852 --> 00:13:32.186
<i>The Rub' al Khali,</i>

00:13:34.188 --> 00:13:35.481
<i>the Empty Quarter.</i>

00:13:45.074 --> 00:13:46.784
<i>The very name of the place</i>

00:13:47.493 --> 00:13:51.289
<i>resonates with the romance</i>
<i>of these desert lands.</i>

00:13:59.339 --> 00:14:03.343
<i>This is the largest sand sea</i>
<i>in the world.</i>

00:14:07.513 --> 00:14:11.768
<i>There are parts</i>
<i>where human beings never venture.</i>

00:14:16.898 --> 00:14:21.069
<i>Only the greatest desert specialists</i>
<i>can survive here.</i>

00:14:27.742 --> 00:14:29.535
<i>Arabian oryx.</i>

00:14:33.998 --> 00:14:35.666
<i>Their ranges are vast,</i>

00:14:36.459 --> 00:14:39.879
<i>extending</i>
<i>for over 3,000 square kilometers.</i>

00:14:43.925 --> 00:14:47.261
<i>This is one of their last refuges.</i>

00:14:52.266 --> 00:14:54.519
<i>Once hunted close to extinction,</i>

00:14:55.269 --> 00:14:58.564
<i>they have now reclaimed</i>
<i>their ancestral territory.</i>

00:15:02.527 --> 00:15:07.156
<i>With the help of conservationists,</i>
<i>they have returned home.</i>

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:15.581
<i>But reintroduction</i>
<i>cannot save all desert animals.</i>

00:15:16.958 --> 00:15:17.959
[elephants huffing]

00:15:28.219 --> 00:15:29.720
<i>Desert elephants.</i>

00:15:34.642 --> 00:15:38.813
<i>Less than 150 survive, here in Namibia.</i>

00:15:43.276 --> 00:15:48.197
<i>This oldest of deserts</i>
<i>is scarred by dry riverbeds,</i>

00:15:48.489 --> 00:15:53.578
<i>carved by water that flows</i>
<i>for only one or two days in a year.</i>

00:15:58.708 --> 00:15:59.959
<i>An adult elephant</i>

00:16:00.168 --> 00:16:06.340
<i>must find up to 200 kilos of food</i>
<i>each and every day if it's not to starve.</i>

00:16:12.513 --> 00:16:17.727
<i>So, for these last survivors,</i>
<i>life is an endless trek.</i>

00:16:23.858 --> 00:16:29.322
<i>The herd is guided by a single old female,</i>
<i>the matriarch.</i>

00:16:32.325 --> 00:16:34.243
<i>She is leading her family</i>

00:16:34.535 --> 00:16:38.080
<i>to a special place</i>
<i>where food should be available</i>

00:16:38.289 --> 00:16:39.874
<i>even in a drought.</i>

00:16:46.464 --> 00:16:49.383
<i>She learned of its existence</i>
<i>from her mother,</i>

00:16:49.884 --> 00:16:51.093
<i>many years ago.</i>

00:16:53.137 --> 00:16:57.099
<i>Now, she's teaching her own calf</i>
<i>how to get there.</i>

00:17:05.024 --> 00:17:08.861
<i>The elephants are not alone</i>
<i>in their search for food.</i>

00:17:14.825 --> 00:17:16.077
<i>Desert lions.</i>

00:17:17.954 --> 00:17:20.748
<i>They are just as rare as the elephants.</i>

00:17:30.216 --> 00:17:33.010
<i>The calf is protected by its mother.</i>

00:17:40.768 --> 00:17:43.020
<i>So the lions let them pass.</i>

00:17:48.776 --> 00:17:52.071
<i>Distant trees are a sign of water.</i>

00:17:53.698 --> 00:17:54.532
[brays]

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:17.763
<i>The riverbed is dry.</i>

00:18:23.144 --> 00:18:26.689
<i>The ana trees, however, are still green.</i>

00:18:34.238 --> 00:18:35.406
<i>But there is a problem.</i>

00:18:38.492 --> 00:18:39.744
<i>At this time of year,</i>

00:18:39.869 --> 00:18:43.998
<i>seedpods from the ana trees</i>
<i>usually litter the ground.</i>

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:49.462
<i>Rich food for elephants.</i>

00:18:53.799 --> 00:18:57.094
<i>But this year, the crop has failed.</i>

00:19:01.349 --> 00:19:06.562
<i>The matriarch</i>
<i>has led her herd here for nothing.</i>

00:19:15.321 --> 00:19:18.824
<i>Even the trees' leafy branches</i>
<i>are out of their reach.</i>

00:19:21.327 --> 00:19:26.540
<i>The family has no option but to move on.</i>

00:19:45.101 --> 00:19:48.562
<i>A bull, standing nearly four meters tall.</i>

00:19:50.606 --> 00:19:52.983
<i>He can reach into the canopy,</i>

00:19:55.069 --> 00:19:57.738
<i>and he could be the solution</i>
<i>to their problem.</i>

00:20:02.118 --> 00:20:07.581
<i>The old female has known him all her life</i>
<i>and turned to him for help before.</i>

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:20.594
<i>Elephants can only survive here</i>

00:20:20.803 --> 00:20:24.056
<i>because of knowledge</i>
<i>passed down over generations.</i>

00:20:26.559 --> 00:20:29.812
<i>But less than 20 matriarchs still survive,</i>

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:34.191
<i>and if their knowledge is lost,</i>

00:20:35.151 --> 00:20:38.529
<i>elephants may no longer</i>
<i>be able to live here.</i>

00:20:47.788 --> 00:20:52.793
<i>Deserts cannot support</i>
<i>large numbers of animals the year round,</i>

00:20:55.129 --> 00:20:58.090
<i>and even those specially adapted</i>
<i>to these conditions</i>

00:20:59.091 --> 00:21:01.218
<i>can only survive in small numbers.</i>

00:21:04.305 --> 00:21:06.307
<i>But on very special occasions,</i>

00:21:08.058 --> 00:21:10.561
<i>deserts are transformed.</i>

00:21:10.644 --> 00:21:12.646
[thunder rumbling]

00:21:17.777 --> 00:21:19.069
<i>Once in a decade,</i>

00:21:20.821 --> 00:21:22.490
<i>there may be a cloudburst.</i>

00:21:33.626 --> 00:21:36.754
<i>A single one can turn the desert green.</i>

00:21:46.847 --> 00:21:48.265
<i>In southern California,</i>

00:21:48.641 --> 00:21:51.477
<i>the change is visible from space.</i>

00:22:03.364 --> 00:22:07.201
<i>Hundreds of square kilometers</i>
<i>suddenly bloom.</i>

00:22:24.844 --> 00:22:27.972
<i>If such transformations become regular,</i>

00:22:28.722 --> 00:22:30.891
<i>a new habitat may develop.</i>

00:22:33.853 --> 00:22:35.062
<i>Grasslands.</i>

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:38.941
<i>One of our planet's</i>
<i>most productive landscapes.</i>

00:22:45.823 --> 00:22:50.536
<i>They support the greatest aggregations</i>
<i>of large animals on Earth.</i>

00:22:53.664 --> 00:22:58.294
<i>The Serengeti sustains herds</i>
<i>of over a million wildebeest.</i>

00:23:03.382 --> 00:23:07.636
<i>They follow the rains</i>
<i>to crop the newly sprung grass.</i>

00:23:21.025 --> 00:23:24.236
<i>These vast herds attract predators.</i>

00:23:37.041 --> 00:23:39.293
<i>Five male cheetahs.</i>

00:23:40.252 --> 00:23:43.714
<i>One of the largest coalitions</i>
<i>ever observed.</i>

00:23:49.386 --> 00:23:54.600
<i>They dominate a territory</i>
<i>of 450 square kilometers.</i>

00:23:59.897 --> 00:24:01.690
<i>They patrol it together,</i>

00:24:04.193 --> 00:24:06.195
<i>and that attracts attention.</i>

00:24:06.278 --> 00:24:07.738
[birds chirruping]

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:41.105
[braying]

00:24:55.995 --> 00:24:58.247
<i>A change of strategy is needed</i>

00:24:59.331 --> 00:25:01.000
<i>if they're to hunt successfully.</i>

00:25:10.968 --> 00:25:12.386
<i>They need cover.</i>

00:25:44.752 --> 00:25:48.756
<i>An adult wildebeest</i>
<i>is a formidable opponent.</i>

00:26:05.481 --> 00:26:08.984
<i>Four of the cheetahs start the stalk,</i>

00:26:09.985 --> 00:26:12.321
<i>walking directly towards the prey.</i>

00:26:15.324 --> 00:26:18.786
<i>The fifth creeps around the side.</i>

00:26:50.192 --> 00:26:54.947
<i>They need to get really close</i>
<i>before making their final sprint.</i>

00:27:07.292 --> 00:27:08.502
<i>They're nearly there.</i>

00:27:44.163 --> 00:27:45.873
<i>All five break cover,</i>

00:27:47.124 --> 00:27:49.585
<i>each cat chasing a different target.</i>

00:28:05.267 --> 00:28:06.310
<i>It's chaos.</i>

00:28:12.316 --> 00:28:16.528
<i>A single cheetah</i>
<i>is not strong enough to defend its prize.</i>

00:28:30.667 --> 00:28:32.461
<i>They must work together.</i>

00:29:14.586 --> 00:29:19.508
<i>These dramas only continue</i>
<i>because the Serengeti is protected,</i>

00:29:20.342 --> 00:29:23.637
<i>and has been for over 65 years.</i>

00:29:26.556 --> 00:29:29.309
<i>But the Serengeti is an exception.</i>

00:29:31.311 --> 00:29:37.901
<i>Across the planet, space for grasslands</i>
<i>has been steadily disappearing.</i>

00:29:38.902 --> 00:29:41.571
[huffs]

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:48.203
<i>A hundred and eighty years ago,</i>
<i>herds of bison, millions strong,</i>

00:29:48.787 --> 00:29:51.581
<i>grazed the Great Plains</i>
<i>of North America.</i>

00:29:55.335 --> 00:30:00.090
<i>They roamed across a prairie</i>
<i>a hundred times larger than the Serengeti.</i>

00:30:07.931 --> 00:30:10.684
<i>This was the true wild west.</i>

00:30:24.656 --> 00:30:26.366
[roaring]

00:30:26.783 --> 00:30:29.911
<i>Every summer,</i>
<i>the males roared their challenges</i>

00:30:31.538 --> 00:30:34.416
<i>and fought for possession of the females.</i>

00:30:55.604 --> 00:31:00.108
<i>As the rut intensified,</i>
<i>the fights became more brutal.</i>

00:31:34.017 --> 00:31:38.980
<i>Today, however,</i>
<i>most of the prairie is silent.</i>

00:31:44.194 --> 00:31:47.364
<i>Humans slaughtered the great herds.</i>

00:31:51.118 --> 00:31:54.454
<i>Less than 30,000 wild bison remain,</i>

00:31:57.332 --> 00:32:01.086
<i>and 90 percent of the prairie</i>
<i>has been lost,</i>

00:32:03.004 --> 00:32:05.298
<i>most of it to agriculture.</i>

00:32:29.448 --> 00:32:32.784
<i>What we eat, and how we produce it,</i>

00:32:33.493 --> 00:32:37.539
<i>will determine the future</i>
<i>of our planet's grasslands.</i>

00:32:45.255 --> 00:32:49.551
<i>Our past could show us</i>
<i>how we can feed ourselves</i>

00:32:49.926 --> 00:32:51.887
<i>and still leave room for nature.</i>

00:32:55.849 --> 00:33:01.396
<i>The ancient hay meadows of Hungary,</i>
<i>still farmed in the traditional way,</i>

00:33:01.897 --> 00:33:05.317
<i>provide habitats</i>
<i>of extraordinary richness.</i>

00:33:17.162 --> 00:33:19.372
<i>Butterflies are abundant.</i>

00:33:22.334 --> 00:33:27.797
<i>One species has</i>
<i>an almost unbelievably complex life cycle.</i>

00:33:32.135 --> 00:33:33.845
<i>The Alcon blue.</i>

00:33:36.389 --> 00:33:41.978
<i>Each female must mate and lay eggs</i>
<i>on just one species of plant,</i>

00:33:42.479 --> 00:33:43.563
<i>the marsh gentian.</i>

00:33:50.529 --> 00:33:53.073
<i>The eggs soon hatch into caterpillars.</i>

00:33:55.825 --> 00:34:00.247
<i>High up on the plants,</i>
<i>they're safe from predators below.</i>

00:34:10.632 --> 00:34:14.678
<i>But then, the caterpillars</i>
<i>do something seemingly suicidal.</i>

00:34:18.807 --> 00:34:23.478
<i>They abseil down on threads of silk</i>
<i>to the ground below...</i>

00:34:25.313 --> 00:34:27.232
<i>and into danger.</i>

00:34:35.073 --> 00:34:38.285
<i>They have no defense</i>
<i>against the marauding ants,</i>

00:34:38.368 --> 00:34:39.578
<i>which carry them off.</i>

00:34:44.249 --> 00:34:48.420
<i>But this is exactly what the caterpillars</i>
<i>need to happen.</i>

00:34:51.214 --> 00:34:55.677
<i>They're producing a scent</i>
<i>like that emitted by an ant larva.</i>

00:35:00.056 --> 00:35:03.768
<i>The ants respond</i>
<i>by taking them back to their nest.</i>

00:35:11.151 --> 00:35:14.863
<i>There, they deposit them</i>
<i>in the colony's brood chamber.</i>

00:35:17.365 --> 00:35:21.870
<i>The purple-colored caterpillars,</i>
<i>lying among the ants' own white larvae,</i>

00:35:22.787 --> 00:35:25.206
<i>give off just the right signals.</i>

00:35:29.919 --> 00:35:32.380
<i>And the nurse ants rush to feed them.</i>

00:35:35.050 --> 00:35:36.301
<i>But there is more.</i>

00:35:38.011 --> 00:35:43.141
<i>The caterpillars now start to mimic</i>
<i>the sounds made by the queen ant,</i>

00:35:46.019 --> 00:35:49.189
<i>and, as a result,</i>
<i>the ants treat them like royalty.</i>

00:35:53.485 --> 00:35:54.944
<i>If food gets short,</i>

00:35:55.028 --> 00:35:58.990
<i>the ants will even feed the caterpillars</i>
<i>instead of their own young.</i>

00:36:01.660 --> 00:36:06.623
<i>They give them such quantities of food</i>
<i>that the caterpillars grow hugely.</i>

00:36:14.339 --> 00:36:19.886
<i>And there, underground, the caterpillars</i>
<i>feed and grow for nearly two years.</i>

00:36:21.137 --> 00:36:27.352
<i>Until, one day,</i>
<i>there is nothing for the ants to feed.</i>

00:36:30.230 --> 00:36:32.232
<i>The caterpillars have pupated.</i>

00:36:41.157 --> 00:36:42.409
<i>But a few weeks later,</i>

00:36:43.243 --> 00:36:46.913
<i>out crawls an Alcon blue butterfly.</i>

00:37:07.225 --> 00:37:09.185
<i>Now, they begin to leave the nest</i>

00:37:09.269 --> 00:37:13.523
<i>that has been their home</i>
<i>for the last 23 months.</i>

00:37:31.082 --> 00:37:33.793
<i>The young adult</i>
<i>makes its way out of the nest</i>

00:37:34.294 --> 00:37:35.795
<i>and clambers up a grass stem.</i>

00:37:36.546 --> 00:37:41.509
<i>Its wings expand</i>
<i>as it prepares to fly off and find a mate.</i>

00:37:46.097 --> 00:37:50.477
<i>This complex life</i>
<i>may be laborsaving for the butterfly,</i>

00:37:50.977 --> 00:37:52.353
<i>but it's risky.</i>

00:37:54.481 --> 00:37:57.859
<i>If anything happened to the ants</i>
<i>or to the gentian,</i>

00:37:58.234 --> 00:38:01.362
<i>the Alcon blue would become extinct.</i>

00:38:16.711 --> 00:38:19.798
<i>Only tiny fragments</i>
<i>of these ancient meadows</i>

00:38:19.881 --> 00:38:21.174
<i>are left in Europe.</i>

00:38:24.052 --> 00:38:26.679
<i>But beyond them to the east,</i>

00:38:27.055 --> 00:38:33.269
<i>once stretched grasslands that extended</i>
<i>for a fifth of the way around the world,</i>

00:38:33.812 --> 00:38:35.897
<i>from Romania to China.</i>

00:38:58.920 --> 00:39:03.216
<i>Here, there are places where,</i>
<i>for mile after endless mile,</i>

00:39:03.842 --> 00:39:06.344
<i>there are no roads or fences.</i>

00:39:11.599 --> 00:39:16.437
<i>Here, where there are no trees,</i>
<i>eagles nest on the ground.</i>

00:39:24.404 --> 00:39:30.326
<i>Once, these eagles would have preyed</i>
<i>on antelope that numbered in millions.</i>

00:39:35.623 --> 00:39:37.584
<i>And some are still here.</i>

00:39:38.960 --> 00:39:43.756
<i>These are saiga, antelope that live</i>
<i>nowhere else in the world.</i>

00:39:46.009 --> 00:39:49.262
<i>Their extraordinary noses</i>
<i>are specially adapted</i>

00:39:49.470 --> 00:39:53.266
<i>to filter out the dust kicked up</i>
<i>by the immense herds</i>

00:39:53.433 --> 00:39:54.601
<i>that once lived here.</i>

00:39:58.313 --> 00:40:00.815
<i>Now, they're critically endangered.</i>

00:40:01.649 --> 00:40:06.112
<i>Poaching and the loss of habitat</i>
<i>have had a devastating impact on them.</i>

00:40:09.449 --> 00:40:13.494
<i>But conservation efforts have recently</i>
<i>started to make a difference.</i>

00:40:16.039 --> 00:40:20.668
<i>There is still hope</i>
<i>for these extraordinary plains dwellers.</i>

00:40:28.468 --> 00:40:33.181
<i>And the proof of this</i>
<i>can be found further east, in Mongolia,</i>

00:40:33.806 --> 00:40:37.477
<i>where the grasslands</i>
<i>still remain largely intact.</i>

00:40:43.107 --> 00:40:45.693
<i>These are Przewalski’s horses.</i>

00:40:47.111 --> 00:40:50.198
<i>Fifty years ago,</i>
<i>they were extinct in the wild,</i>

00:40:51.199 --> 00:40:54.077
<i>but a few adults survived in captivity.</i>

00:41:00.249 --> 00:41:03.670
<i>Careful breeding from 12 of them</i>
<i>increased their numbers</i>

00:41:03.920 --> 00:41:06.965
<i>until there were sufficient</i>
<i>to release on the plains.</i>

00:41:09.759 --> 00:41:11.886
<i>These are their descendants.</i>

00:41:22.021 --> 00:41:24.941
<i>A stallion protects each harem.</i>

00:41:29.570 --> 00:41:34.283
<i>They must be vigilant,</i>
<i>and they race to defend their herd...</i>

00:41:43.501 --> 00:41:46.629
<i>or chase off bachelors</i>
<i>trying to lure away a mare.</i>

00:42:01.269 --> 00:42:03.688
<i>With their numbers now topping 300,</i>

00:42:05.106 --> 00:42:08.651
<i>the future for these wild horses</i>
<i>looks more secure.</i>

00:42:12.864 --> 00:42:14.991
<i>Their recovery was only possible</i>

00:42:15.408 --> 00:42:20.663
<i>because the vast Mongolian steppe</i>
<i>still remains largely untouched.</i>

00:42:33.217 --> 00:42:39.307
<i>These grasses are some of the tallest</i>
<i>to be found anywhere on our planet.</i>

00:42:42.727 --> 00:42:47.315
<i>They're so tall</i>
<i>they can conceal elephants.</i>

00:42:56.365 --> 00:43:00.286
<i>They make the giants that live among them</i>
<i>seem small.</i>

00:43:19.305 --> 00:43:21.224
<i>A last hiding place</i>

00:43:21.307 --> 00:43:25.686
<i>for the highly endangered</i>
<i>greater one-horned rhino.</i>

00:43:36.489 --> 00:43:37.990
<i>This is India,</i>

00:43:39.742 --> 00:43:42.286
<i>one of the most populous countries</i>
<i>on Earth.</i>

00:43:45.289 --> 00:43:51.420
<i>Yet here there is a great determination</i>
<i>to protect these crucial grasslands.</i>

00:44:06.602 --> 00:44:08.146
<i>What must it be like</i>

00:44:08.396 --> 00:44:12.191
<i>to live in this dense,</i>
<i>claustrophobic world?</i>

00:44:21.909 --> 00:44:25.997
<i>Just moving about</i>
<i>could mean walking into danger.</i>

00:44:41.679 --> 00:44:46.309
<i>The grasses conceal tigers.</i>

00:44:57.069 --> 00:45:00.573
<i>Stripes and shadows blend.</i>

00:45:14.545 --> 00:45:18.925
<i>Long grass</i>
<i>may hide a tigress from her prey,</i>

00:45:24.972 --> 00:45:28.809
<i>but it also hides the prey from her.</i>

00:45:37.235 --> 00:45:40.446
<i>She must get within 20 meters of it.</i>

00:45:46.619 --> 00:45:51.540
<i>And she must always know</i>
<i>exactly where the prey are hidden.</i>

00:46:42.800 --> 00:46:44.593
<i>She may have lost them.</i>

00:47:06.907 --> 00:47:09.118
<i>She risks a look.</i>

00:47:12.997 --> 00:47:14.123
[bleats]

00:47:17.543 --> 00:47:19.545
[bleating resonates]

00:47:38.314 --> 00:47:44.403
<i>Every deer around</i>
<i>now knows exactly where the tiger is.</i>

00:47:58.751 --> 00:48:02.713
<i>Others have heard the signal</i>
<i>that announced her failure.</i>

00:48:11.847 --> 00:48:12.807
<i>Her cubs.</i>

00:48:22.566 --> 00:48:25.236
<i>She had left them hidden in the grass.</i>

00:48:28.614 --> 00:48:29.949
[growling]

00:48:39.625 --> 00:48:41.710
<i>In the last hundred years,</i>

00:48:42.086 --> 00:48:47.800
<i>the number of wild tigers</i>
<i>has declined by over 95 percent.</i>

00:48:51.554 --> 00:48:52.972
<i>But here in India,</i>

00:48:53.722 --> 00:48:56.434
<i>despite the enormous pressure</i>
<i>from poaching,</i>

00:48:57.518 --> 00:48:59.562
<i>and a growing human population,</i>

00:49:01.063 --> 00:49:04.066
<i>tiger numbers are actually increasing.</i>

00:49:13.451 --> 00:49:18.581
<i>Protect the precious space</i>
<i>that grasslands and deserts provide,</i>

00:49:21.167 --> 00:49:24.170
<i>and the animals will bounce back.</i>

00:49:40.519 --> 00:49:42.730
<i>Please visit ourplanet.com</i>

00:49:43.063 --> 00:49:47.902
<i>to discover what we need to do now</i>
<i>to protect wild grasslands.</i>

00:49:49.945 --> 00:49:55.826
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Singing together ♪</i>

00:49:58.454 --> 00:50:04.418
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say it's now or never ♪</i>

00:50:07.213 --> 00:50:11.509
<i>♪ 'Cause it's not too late</i>
<i>If we change our ways ♪</i>

00:50:11.592 --> 00:50:15.054
<i>♪ And connect the dots to our problems ♪</i>

00:50:15.471 --> 00:50:21.143
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say we're in this together ♪</i>

00:50:21.602 --> 00:50:23.312
<i>♪ We're in this together ♪</i>